  "query_preview": "..\nThe weather pattern across the Marianas is influenced by a very \nbroad trough and embedded weak low to the west, the remnants of \nInvest 90W to the east-southeast, and a developing monsoon pattern \nacross western Micronesia. Scatterometry from this morning shows an \narea of light to gentle south-southwest to south winds across the \ncoastal waters. Winds remain light to gentle tonight as they \ngradually shift southeasterly, increasing slightly tomorrow, which \nwill push more of the island-effect showers off-shore in the \nafternoon. Models then depict a complex pattern unfolding over the \nnext few days as the monsoon pattern strengthens south of the \nislands, and a trough looks to approach from the northeast. These \nfeatures will interact to generate scattered showers around Tuesday \nnight and Wednesday, as the overall monsoon pattern begins to lift \nnorthwest. Within the eastern periphery of this monsoon pattern, \nmodel guidance depicts the formation of a broad disturbance south to \nsouthwest of the Marianas in the latter half of the week. As this \nfeature moves northwest and passes near or southwest of the area, at \nleast scattered showers are expected, continuing into the weekend. \nThere is a large degree of uncertainty in how the pattern will evolve\nin the coming days. If this feature develops or passes closer to the\nislands, showers may become numerous to widespread at times, and \nwinds could become breezy and gusty. For now, models depict the \ndisturbance developing farther west of the Marianas, keeping the \nislands along its eastern periphery.",
